            'body_en' => "The minaret was begun in 586 of the Hijra — 1190 or 1191 — and finished in 630, that is 1232 or 1233: the two dates frame almost exactly the reign of Muzaffar al-Din Gökböri, who entered Erbil as its lord in January 1191 and died in 1233, and whose name it has carried ever since. It is believed to be all that survives of one of his mosques; the building itself vanished centuries ago, and the tower was left standing in the fields west of the citadel, where the city has since grown around it and made it a park.\n\nIts form is the classic one of the Seljuk and Ayyubid centuries in Mesopotamia: an octagonal base about a third of the height, then a round shaft that tapers as it climbs, with a balcony on corbels between the two. A door on the east side of the base opens onto a stair that spirals up inside the thickness of the wall to the balcony, and on to a second balcony that has since collapsed. The whole is built of fired brick, the material of every great building of the region from Samarra to the citadel — and here the brick is not only structure but ornament.\n\nTwo Kufic inscriptions name its builders, Muhammad and Mas'ud; the inscription that would have named the mosque is gone with the mosque.",
